# libpinyin.alloc — the allocator-pairing register for the pinyin C ABI.
#
# Every pointer-shaped slot the frozen `pinyin.h` declares — a pointer
# return, or a `T **` out-parameter — is classified here exactly once, and
# `tools/abi/check-alloc-pairing.sh` proves three things against it: that
# the register covers the header's slot set with nothing missing and
# nothing extra; that each class agrees with the C type the header gives
# the slot; and, by running a C++ consumer built from this file under
# LeakSanitizer, that the declared deallocator is the one that actually
# releases the allocation.
#
# Sibling of `libpinyin.ver`, and read the same way: the .ver file freezes
# WHICH symbols cross the boundary, this one freezes WHAT CROSSES WITH
# THEM. A new entry point with an out-pointer fails the gate until it is
# classified here.
#
# Format — five whitespace-separated fields, `#` comments, blank lines
# ignored:
#
#   <symbol>  <slot>  <class>  <notes>  <verified>
#
#   <slot>   `return`, or the parameter's name as `pinyin.h` spells it.
#   <class>  one of:
#     handle:<fn>  an opaque handle; <fn> is the ONLY function that may
#                  release it, and must itself be an exported symbol.
#     g_free       a `gchar *` / `char *` the caller owns and releases
#                  with `g_free`. Allocated by `ffi::owned_cstr`, i.e.
#                  libc `malloc`: g_free has been a thin `free` wrapper
#                  since GLib 2.46 removed the memory vtable, so the two
#                  allocators are the same one. This is the pairing
#                  assumption the dynamic half of the gate exercises.
#     g_strfreev   a NULL-terminated `gchar **` the caller owns; both the
#                  array and every string come from `ffi::owned_cstr_list`
#                  (libc `malloc`), and `g_strfreev` g_free's each.
#     borrowed     owned by the context or instance and released with it.
#                  The caller must never free it.
#   <notes>  What the slot holds when the call answers `false` — the part
#            of the contract a consumer gets wrong silently. Each value is
#            EXECUTABLE: `tools/abi/alloc-pairing-*.cc` drives the failure
#            path and asserts the stated state, and the gate rejects a
#            register entry whose contract was never probed. "Reachable"
#            means reachable by a CONFORMING consumer — valid handles and
#            in-contract arguments, an empty parse or an exhausted
#            iterator — never a NULL-argument refusal, which is caller
#            misuse and where the pin would simply crash.
#     false-allocates    the call can return `false` HAVING ALLOCATED, so a
#                        consumer that frees only on `true` leaks. Upstream
#                        parity, not an oxpinyin choice (`pinyin.cpp:3382-
#                        3386, 3442-3445, 904-910`).
#     false-nulls        the out-param is NULL on the reachable `false`.
#     false-untouched    the out-param is left as the caller set it, so the
#                        caller must initialize it.
#     false-unreachable  the call has failure paths, but none a conforming
#                        consumer can reach, so no out-param state is
#                        claimed. An explicit claim about reachability, not
#                        a skip: challenge it in review like any other.
#     n/a                the slot is a return value.
#   <verified>  How <notes> was established. The gate checks that every
#            line carries one of these kinds, never the evidence it names:
#     pin-probe:<case>       measured at the pin and on oxpinyin, identical,
#                            on the reference cell (bdb) by <case>: a
#                            `tools/bisection/contract-diff.py` case, or a
#                            runner in `tools/bisection/`.
#     pin-aborts:row<N>      the path the gate probes aborts the pin; <notes>
#                            is oxpinyin's answer there, registered as row
#                            <N> of `docs/findings/compatibility-policy.md`.
#     source:<file>:<lines>  argued from the pin's `src/<file>` at 074a2219.
#     unverified             executed against oxpinyin only, never compared
#                            with the pin.
#     n/a                    <notes> is n/a.
#
# Corrected 2026-09-10 when the notes became executable: six slots were
# described from a reading of the code rather than from running it, and
# three of those were wrong — pinyin_get_candidate, pinyin_get_pinyin_key
# and pinyin_get_pinyin_key_rest all NULL their out-param on the reachable
# refusal, where this file said they left it untouched.
#
# Corrected 2026-10-08 (#681): the bigram iterator's two slots said
# `false-untouched`, but its terminal row answers `false` having written
# both strings, so they are `false-allocates`. <verified> was added the
# same day.

# ── Handles: allocated by Box::into_raw, released by their destructor ──
pinyin_init                              return      handle:pinyin_fini            n/a                n/a
pinyin_alloc_instance                    return      handle:pinyin_free_instance   n/a                n/a
pinyin_begin_add_phrases                 return      handle:pinyin_end_add_phrases n/a                n/a
pinyin_begin_get_phrases                 return      handle:pinyin_end_get_phrases n/a                n/a
pinyin_begin_get_bigram_phrases          return      handle:pinyin_end_get_bigram_phrases n/a                n/a

# ── Borrowed pointers: never freed by the caller ──────────────────────
# The context the instance was allocated from — the same pointer
# `pinyin_init` returned, not a second handle.
pinyin_get_context                       return      borrowed                      n/a                n/a
pinyin_get_candidate                     candidate   borrowed                      false-nulls        unverified
# The candidate string has no `false` at the pin: it stores the pointer and
# answers `true` (pinyin.cpp:2873-2878).
pinyin_get_candidate_string              utf8_str    borrowed                      false-unreachable  source:pinyin.cpp:2873-2878
pinyin_get_pinyin_key                    key         borrowed                      false-nulls        pin-aborts:row63
pinyin_get_pinyin_key_rest               key_rest    borrowed                      false-nulls        pin-aborts:row63

# ── Caller-owned strings (g_free) ─────────────────────────────────────
pinyin_get_sentence                      sentence    g_free                        false-untouched    pin-probe:sentence-before-guess
pinyin_get_full_pinyin_auxiliary_text    aux_text    g_free                        false-allocates    pin-probe:aux-text-no-parse
pinyin_get_double_pinyin_auxiliary_text  aux_text    g_free                        false-allocates    pin-probe:aux-text-no-parse
pinyin_get_chewing_auxiliary_text        aux_text    g_free                        false-allocates    pin-probe:aux-text-no-parse
pinyin_get_pinyin_string                 utf8_str    g_free                        false-unreachable  unverified
pinyin_get_zhuyin_string                 utf8_str    g_free                        false-unreachable  unverified
pinyin_get_luoma_pinyin_string           utf8_str    g_free                        false-unreachable  unverified
pinyin_get_secondary_zhuyin_string       utf8_str    g_free                        false-unreachable  unverified
# The table_index == 0 refusal, the only non-NULL-argument `false` these
# reach, needs an unset ChewingKey — and `ChewingKey` is an opaque typedef,
# so a conforming consumer cannot make one. Unreachable, not unclaimed.
pinyin_get_pinyin_strings                shengmu     g_free                        false-unreachable  unverified
pinyin_get_pinyin_strings                yunmu       g_free                        false-unreachable  unverified
pinyin_token_get_phrase                  utf8_str    g_free                        false-untouched    pin-probe:token-get-phrase-out
# The unigram iterator answers `true` on every row it hands out
# (pinyin.cpp:698-769); its `false` is the call with no next row, where the
# pin asserts (:709) and oxpinyin answers without writing.
pinyin_iterator_get_next_phrase          phrase      g_free                        false-untouched    pin-aborts:row62
pinyin_iterator_get_next_phrase          pinyin      g_free                        false-untouched    pin-aborts:row62
# The bigram iterator answers has_next after taking a row
# (pinyin.cpp:904-910), so the row after which has_next is false comes back
# `false` having written both strings, and a loop that frees only on `true`
# leaks it. Called with no row pending, the pin asserts (:902) or reads NULL
# (:905); oxpinyin answers `false` there without writing.
pinyin_bigram_iterator_get_next_phrase   phrase      g_free                        false-allocates    pin-probe:run-bigram-export-diff.sh
pinyin_bigram_iterator_get_next_phrase   pinyin      g_free                        false-allocates    pin-probe:run-bigram-export-diff.sh

# ── Caller-owned string vectors (g_strfreev) ──────────────────────────
pinyin_in_chewing_keyboard               symbols     g_strfreev                    false-nulls        unverified
